   "id": "0e2c3eef-665c-46f5-a7c1-8a777e90a2f2",
   "term": "Groove-depth control",
   "verdict": "MINOR",
   "suggestions": [
    {
     "field": "plain_english",
     "severity": "Low",
     "issue": "The analogy for a vertical-depth concept ('going shallower to save room... like writing smaller where the page is nearly full') maps naturally onto horizontal packing, which is exactly what pitch/spacing does. Since this entry's own common_mistakes warns learners NOT to confuse depth (vertical) with pitch (horizontal), the analogy quietly reinforces the misconception it later tries to correct.",
     "suggestion": "Recast the analogy around vertical cut effort/space, e.g. 'like carving a shallower channel where the sound is quiet so less material is removed,' and let the 'more music per side' benefit be attributed to depth working WITH pitch, rather than to a horizontal-packing image."
    }
